original_candidate.explanation:
Sequential storage supports constant-time indexed access to each record and its neighbors. Ideal first-order monomer consumption follows dM/dt = −kM. Separating variables and integrating gives ln(M(t)/M0) = −kt, hence M(t) = M0 exp(−kt). Applying this relationship to adjacent records gives k_i = ln(M_(i−1)/M_i)/(t_i−t_(i−1)). These are rate constants, not untransformed monomer-consumption rates. All three interval constants equal ln(2) min⁻¹. The conversions are 0, 0.5, 0.75 and 0.875; their increments are positive. The adjacent Xn–p slopes are 100, 100 and 100. Thus positivity and both 1% relative-tolerance tests pass throughout. Ideal RDRP also requires a narrow molecular-weight distribution, so the independently measured broad distribution produces a batch-level failure.

Pseudocode for the supplied positive-concentration, time-ordered records:

```text
function screen(records, distribution):
    M0 = records[0].M
    for i = 1 to length(records) - 1:
        previous = records[i - 1]
        current = records[i]
        dt = current.t - previous.t
        p_previous = 1 - previous.M / M0
        p_current = 1 - current.M / M0
        dp = p_current - p_previous
        k = ln(previous.M / current.M) / dt
        if k <= 0 or dp <= 0:
            return ("record failure", i + 1)
        slope = (current.Xn - previous.Xn) / dp
        if slope <= 0:
            return ("record failure", i + 1)
        if i == 1:
            k_reference = k
            slope_reference = slope
        if abs(k - k_reference) > 0.01 * k_reference:
            return ("record failure", i + 1)
        if abs(slope - slope_reference) > 0.01 * slope_reference:
            return ("record failure", i + 1)
    if distribution != "narrow":
        return "batch-level failure"
    return "acceptance"
```

The traversal uses zero-based indices, so returning i + 1 reports the later record's one-based position in the LocateElem convention.

A sequential list provides constant-time indexed access to a record and its indexed neighbors. A doubly linked list supports local neighbor access but generally requires linear traversal to locate an arbitrary indexed record, so it is not the preferred representation for the stated workload. For first-order monomer consumption, dM/dt = −kM. Separation and integration give ln(M/M0) = −kt and M(t) = M0 exp(−kt). Thus the adjacent-interval estimate is k_i = ln(M_(i−1)/M_i)/(t_i−t_(i−1)). Both intervals give ln(2) ≈ 0.693147 min⁻¹. The conversions are 0, 0.5, and 0.75, giving positive increments and identical positive Xn–p slopes of 100. All interval comparisons pass. Broad distribution then fails the narrow-distribution characteristic of ideal RDRP, yielding a distribution-only failure.

